  "question": {
    "text": "The lanthanide ion that would show colour is:"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The elements or ions having unpaired electrons show colour. The valence shell electronic configuration of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>La</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Lu</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>14</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;respectively. Hence, they are colourless.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Gd</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;is also colourless,&nbsp;this is because, here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi></math>&nbsp;orbitals are half filled,hence stable.As such <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi></math> transitions are not possible because they will destroy the symmetry of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi></math>&nbsp;and make them unstable.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Sm</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;has the valence shell electronic configuration&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></math>, hence, it is coloured due to the presence of unpaired electrons.</p><p><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo></math> Colourless<br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo></math> Colourless<br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo></math> Pale yellow colour<br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo></math> Colourless</p>"
  }
}